In one Continual-ARC task, demo 1 mapped a 3x13 input to 9x26 output (a 3x expansion in height and 2x in width), while demo 2 mapped an 8x1 input to 24x2. A speculative construction based on geometric repetition was submitted and rejected. The immediate follow-up requested another demo without first resolving the contradictions in the existing examples.

Reusable lesson: dimensions suggest candidate scale factors but do not establish how source values map to output cells. Inspect outputs block-by-block or by sequence order, test expansion, reflow, and other candidate mappings against every cell in all demos, and state a concrete unresolved distinction before spending another demo request. Do not label a mapping validated unless it reproduces each demonstration exactly.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-mapping/references/ambiguous-output-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-rejection-ledger", "file": "references/color-mask-recovery.md", "content": "# Avoiding unsupported cellwise color masks

This session's input had three vertical zones separated by a solid marker column. The demo mapped a 27×21 grid to a 27×10 result, preserving a selected compact region; output colors encoded a summary rather than a direct recoloring of every test input cell. Two rejected predictions instead produced an 18×11 direct color transformation of the left-side values. The second attempt changed which source color was treated as selected without validating that mapping against the demonstrated output.

For future separator/summary puzzles, explicitly map demo coordinates and region boundaries to output coordinates and compare all rows before predicting. Output width and separator position are clues, not proof of a simple crop. Do not transfer a direct cellwise mask unless it exactly reproduces the demo. After rejection, revisit the representation and mapping rather than changing only a color-selection assumption.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-rejection-ledger/references/color-mask-recovery.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-attempt-ledger", "file": "references/post-rejection-structural-audit.md", "content": "# Post-rejection recovery and grid integrity

After an ARC rejection, treat the submitted interpretation as falsified rather than trying successive speculative variants. Preserve a brief ledger of the hypothesis and changed cells, revisit the complete demo set, and test each remaining rule against both transformed and untouched candidates. Request another demo only when a named distinction could change the test prediction; otherwise submit only a rule that exactly explains all available demos.

Before submission, separately audit construction integrity: exact row count, identical intended width for every row, values restricted to 0–9, and unchanged-cell preservation. Structural validity and transformation correctness are separate checks; passing one does not imply the other. In an active budgeted puzzle, avoid unrelated tool/reference calls—use each action for evidence, discriminating sampling, or validated output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545205/attempts/52b150a3511a4455a56f31d31c01d91b/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-attempt-ledger/references/post-rejection-structural-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-task-solving", "file": "references/rejected-submission-rederivation.md", "content": "# Re-derivation after rejected submissions

An incorrect ARC submission falsifies the submitted interpretation; it is not a cue to tweak a minor detail and try again. Return to the complete demo input/output delta, inventory changed and unchanged candidate objects, and compare competing rule hypotheses. Validate a candidate against every changed cell and tempting unchanged lookalike before transfer. If the existing pair cannot distinguish hypotheses that lead to different test outputs, request a targeted demonstration rather than submitting a speculative visual match. Avoid no-op tool calls: use each limited action for analysis, a discriminating demo request, or a validated submission.
